WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.929
This machine may hold the secrets to time.

00:00:05.005 --> 00:00:07.452
Inside it, scientists are looking for the mysterious

00:00:07.452 --> 00:00:10.290
mechanism that controls the daily biological clock.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:15.240
Everything in your body is controlled on this 24-hour process.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:16.770
The things that seems most

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:17.970
interesting to me about

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:19.016
circadian biology

00:00:19.016 --> 00:00:21.210
is the fact that it spans many timescales

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:22.712
and many size scales.

00:00:22.712 --> 00:00:24.287
For more than a quarter of a century,

00:00:24.287 --> 00:00:26.550
chronobiologist, Dr. Carrie Partch,

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:29.138
has been peering inside our biological clock mechanisms

00:00:29.138 --> 00:00:32.070
trying to understand just what makes them tick.

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:35.070
I mean, I was hooked. As soon as I began graduate school.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.382
I really knew that I wanted to work to unravel

00:00:37.382 --> 00:00:40.080
the molecular details of circadian timing.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:43.563
In 2020, Dr. Partch was diagnosed

00:00:43.563 --> 00:00:45.360
with a neurodegenerative disease,

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:49.110
ALS, also known as Lou Gehrig's disease.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:54.030
Due the ALS, my speech is beginning to decline,

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.760
so I use an AI clone of my voice,

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.700
to give talks and to answer questions like this.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.897
But she's still in search of answers to how the clocks in our cells

00:01:02.897 --> 00:01:04.710
govern our days and nights.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.619
I believe we all need a purpose. I can't imagine not working.

00:01:08.619 --> 00:01:11.974
Also, I really enjoy the moment of discovery in research

00:01:11.974 --> 00:01:14.445
after putting in lots of thought and hard work

00:01:14.445 --> 00:01:17.720
to be awarded with a fundamental insight into life is

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.110
about as good as it gets.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:25.170
Chronobiologists study the effect of time on biological cycles.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:27.307
Those could be as fast as a heartbeat

00:01:27.307 --> 00:01:30.000
or slow as a seasonal animal migration.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:32.865
There's one rhythm most of us know
intimately:

00:01:32.865 --> 00:01:34.290
the circadian rhythm,

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.950
which governs a living organism's 24-hour clock.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:42.965
This daily synchronization was first observed in the early 1700s

00:01:42.965 --> 00:01:45.922
when a French astronomer put a plant in a dark room

00:01:45.922 --> 00:01:48.947
and watched it respond to the cycles of day and night.

00:01:48.947 --> 00:01:52.190
Since then, researchers have been trying to decipher

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:54.689
just what sets the rhythm of these clocks.

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:57.445
Advances in DNA sequencing in the
1990s

00:01:57.445 --> 00:02:00.152
led to breakthroughs that began to unravel the mystery,

00:02:00.152 --> 00:02:03.120
by identifying the genes that govern circadian rhythms.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:07.621
In 2017,  a trio of researchers were awarded the Nobel Prize

00:02:07.621 --> 00:02:10.098
for their work on the molecular mechanisms

00:02:10.098 --> 00:02:12.240
controlling fruit fly circadian rhythms.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:15.753
Identifying the genes required for timekeeping

00:02:15.753 --> 00:02:18.718
and understanding how they work together in a genetic network

00:02:18.718 --> 00:02:21.450
was an incredible step forward for our field.

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:24.437
That was the end of the story for
many scientists,

00:02:24.437 --> 00:02:25.860
but not for all.

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:30.020
For people like Carrie and me and other people in structural biology,

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:32.790
there is another layer that I think we really want to know.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:36.516
I'm really interested in the molecular
steps

00:02:36.516 --> 00:02:38.194
of these biological clocks

00:02:38.194 --> 00:02:40.351
and how protein-based signals in our cells

00:02:40.351 --> 00:02:41.700
can measure out a day.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.700
The circadian clock is triggered by the sun,

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.220
but it's not just about sleeping and waking.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:51.570
The circadian clock also governs more than 40% of human genes,

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:55.440
which regulate myriad other essential functions, including metabolism,

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:56.626
hormone production,

00:02:56.626 --> 00:02:58.992
and body temperature over the course of the day.

00:02:58.992 --> 00:03:01.884
In fact, some medicines like acetaminophen

00:03:01.884 --> 00:03:03.880
are better metabolized in the daytime.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:05.681
And vaccines can work
differently

00:03:05.681 --> 00:03:07.750
depending on when they're given in the morning

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:08.917
or at night.

00:03:08.917 --> 00:03:10.810
Many aspects of modern life

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:12.722
like traveling across time zones

00:03:12.722 --> 00:03:14.820
and exposure to artificial light at night

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:17.860
can wreak havoc on our natural circadian clocks.

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:20.302
But being out of sync with the natural
cycles

00:03:20.302 --> 00:03:22.240
can cause more than just fatigue.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:24.543
Working the overnight shift

00:03:24.543 --> 00:03:26.920
is associated with diabetes and heart disease,

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:28.848
as well as certain cancers.

00:03:28.848 --> 00:03:32.142
To fix this mismatch between our bodies and the modern world,

00:03:32.142 --> 00:03:35.320
Partch is searching for ways to tweak the circadian clock.

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:37.510
But first, she had to map it.

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:41.590
For us to understand sort of how the gears of the clock turn,

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:44.024
we need to understand how proteins find one another,

00:03:44.024 --> 00:03:45.531
how they interact,

00:03:45.531 --> 00:03:47.125
how long they stay together,

00:03:47.125 --> 00:03:49.452
and how that might be changed over time.

00:03:49.452 --> 00:03:52.810
To study the human biological clock at the molecular level,

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:53.460
Partch's lab uses a technique called Nuclear Magnetic Resonance Spectroscopy

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:57.640
or NMR.

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:03.220
NMR is very highly related to MRI,

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:05.680
which many people will know a lot more about.

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:08.555
What NMR and MRI have in common

00:04:08.555 --> 00:04:10.180
is we both need really big magnets.

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:13.311
In an NMR tank, liquid nitrogen and helium,

00:04:13.311 --> 00:04:14.920
cool a superconducting magnet,

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:18.910
which produces a magnetic field a
million times stronger than Earth's.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:20.920
So why do we do all that?

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:24.674
That high magnetic field lets us be
able

00:04:24.674 --> 00:04:26.290
to detect signals coming off

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:28.928
of many of the atoms inside of the proteins

00:04:28.928 --> 00:04:31.740
or drugs or DNA or RNA

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:34.240
that we're putting inside of a tube in the middle of the magnet.

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:39.392
To study the biochemical and biophysical mechanisms

00:04:39.392 --> 00:04:40.825
of the circadian rhythms,

00:04:40.825 --> 00:04:44.380
we need to make proteins outside
the human body in test tubes.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:45.741
We take the DNA

00:04:45.741 --> 00:04:47.498
and we tell bacteria to do that for us.

00:04:47.498 --> 00:04:48.580
So this is the NMR tube,

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:50.890
we put it in the magnet

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:52.330
to take the reading.

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:56.920
Using NMR, researchers like Partch can see a picture of proteins at work.

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:01.678
NMR is sort of like taking a series of GPS snapshots of a protein.

00:05:01.678 --> 00:05:06.020
NMR gives us information about the environment around certain atoms

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:07.630
in the protein and how they move,

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:11.623
which lets us infer how the protein wiggles around in solution

00:05:11.623 --> 00:05:15.520
and responds to its partners like other proteins or drugs.

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:17.953
The real power of it is that all the work we do is

00:05:17.953 --> 00:05:21.130
literally on samples in solution at room temperature.

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:25.900
In Partch's lab, the samples are often
of what's called 'clock proteins.'

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:30.490
We call the proteins that interact with
each other and our genes to regulate our

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:32.800
circadian rhythms 'clock proteins',

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:36.310
just like how the gears of a mechanical clock work together.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:41.740
At the heart of the process are two protein molecules called CLOCK and BMAL.

00:05:42.490 --> 00:05:43.830
Each day at dawn

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:47.801
CLOCK and BMAL pair up in the cell cytoplasm before entering the nucleus.

00:05:47.801 --> 00:05:49.210
Once inside,

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:53.290
they bind to the DNA on thousands
of sites to regulate the production of

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:55.690
proteins important for daytime functions.

00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:57.551
The CLOCK-BMAL pair

00:05:57.551 --> 00:06:02.151
also regulates the production of two repressor proteins PERIOD and CRY,

00:06:02.168 --> 00:06:05.616
which play a critical role in their circadian feedback loop.

00:06:05.616 --> 00:06:08.840
Levels of PERIOD and CRY build up
during the day.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:12.821
Then at night,  they enter the nucleus to strip CLOCK-BMAL from the DNA,

00:06:12.821 --> 00:06:13.966
shutting things down.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:16.970
Over the course of the twilight hours,

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:20.210
the repressor proteins decay, diminishing their effect.

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:23.661
While the supply of CLOCK and BMAL
proteins builds back up.

00:06:23.661 --> 00:06:27.035
In the morning, the whole cycle begins again.

00:06:27.052 --> 00:06:31.210
However, genetic variations can alter this protein clock cycle,

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:35.090
leaving some out of sync with the day and night light cycles of Earth.

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:38.600
One tiny change in a clock gene,

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:43.250
an inherited change that only alters
a few atoms in the protein it encodes

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:46.343
can dramatically alter your internal
clock timing

00:06:46.343 --> 00:06:48.493
and change when you go to bed and wake up.

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:51.077
I'm sure we all know some morning-larks,

00:06:51.077 --> 00:06:54.358
folks who go to bed early and enjoy getting up with the sun

00:06:54.358 --> 00:06:56.870
or night owls who like staying up late.

00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:00.660
While some genetic changes can change clock timing by hours

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:04.190
leading to bedtimes at 5:00 PM or 4:00 AM.

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:08.000
By studying the smallest components of our biological clocks,

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:10.964
Partch's lab is searching for ways to correct problems

00:07:10.964 --> 00:07:13.974
caused by genetics or the intrusion of the modern world

00:07:13.974 --> 00:07:15.170
on our circadian rhythms.

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:18.572
We're using NMR now to discover and map

00:07:18.572 --> 00:07:21.450
how small molecules bind to clock proteins

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:23.731
with the hope of turning these
into drugs

00:07:23.731 --> 00:07:27.110
that could help people adjust to shift work or reduce jet lag

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:29.162
or even treat certain cancers

00:07:29.162 --> 00:07:32.623
like glioblastoma that depend on circadian machinery.

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:35.973
To Partch,  the world is full of clocks

00:07:35.973 --> 00:07:38.030
waiting to be pulled apart and understood.

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:42.079
Oh, there is still so much more to learn about our own clocks

00:07:42.079 --> 00:07:45.266
and circadian rhythms in other species like plants

00:07:45.276 --> 00:07:48.493
that could be important for sustaining life on Earth

00:07:48.493 --> 00:07:49.190
and beyond.

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:53.574
It's exciting to see an awareness of circadian biology

00:07:53.574 --> 00:07:57.200
and its impacts on our health finally begin to grow in the clinic.

00:07:57.860 --> 00:07:59.471
Hopefully we can build on this

00:07:59.471 --> 00:08:03.701
to incorporate a better understanding of how time influences our biology

00:08:03.701 --> 00:08:07.850
and improve everything from our sleep to how we respond to drugs.

